e probability of getting a medal on the finals if the team has i participants of previous finals, and other n ΓÇë-ΓÇë i participants arrived to the finals for the first time.\n\nDespite such useful data, the university governors are unable to determine such team forming tactics that would provide the maximum probability of winning a medal at ACM ICPC v2.0 finals on average (we are supposed to want to provide such result to the far future and we are also supposed to have an endless supply of students). And how about you, can you offer such optimal tactic? At the first stage the university governors want to know the value of maximum average probability.\n\nMore formally, suppose that the university sends a team to the k -th world finals. The team has a k participants of previous finals ( 0ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë a k ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ). Since each person can participate in the finals no more than twice, the following condition must be true: . Your task is to choose sequence so that the limit ╬¿ exists and it's value is maximal:\n \nAs is an infinite sequence, you should only print the maximum value of the ╬¿ limit.\n\n## Input\n\nThe first line contains an integer n ( 3ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë100 ), n is the number of team participants. The second line contains n ΓÇë+ΓÇë1 real numbers with no more than 6 digits after decimal point p i ( 0ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë i ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ,ΓÇë0ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë p i ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë1 ) ΓÇö the probability of that the team will win a medal if it contains i participants who has already been on the finals. Also the condition p i ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë p i ΓÇë+ΓÇë1 should be fulfilled for all 0ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë i ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ΓÇë-ΓÇë1 .\n\n## Output\n\nPrint the only real number ΓÇö the expected average number of medals won per year if the optimal strategy is used. The result may have absolute or relative error 10 ΓÇë-ΓÇë6 .\n\n## Examples\n\nExample 1:\n```\n3\n0.115590 0.384031 0.443128 0.562356\n```\n```\n0.4286122500\n```\n\n## Note\n\nIn the second test, no matter what participants the team contains, it 